        title: 'Grandma’s Rocking Horse',
        body: 'The rocking horse had been in Grandma Lorraine’s attic since 1974. It was painted red with a yarn mane, and every grandchild in the family had worn the paint off its ears. When the cousins came for Thanksgiving, nine-year-old Jalen dragged it down to the living room and rode it so hard the china cabinet rattled.\n\nHis older sister Imani, home from her first semester of college, sat on the couch scrolling through a planner app that had more color codes than the subway map. She had joined six clubs, taken a work-study job, signed up for a mission trip and a step team, and she was, as she told her mother on the phone twice a week, exhausted and not sure why she was there.\n\nGrandma watched both of them for a while. Then she said, Jalen, baby, where you going?\n\nTexas! Jalen shouted, rocking harder.\n\nHow far you got?\n\nJalen stopped. He looked at the carpet. Same place, he admitted.\n\nGrandma laughed and turned to Imani. That’s what Dr. Munroe used to say about a life without purpose. Much motion, no progress. You been riding a rocking horse since August, sweetheart.\n\nImani put the phone down. I’m doing good things, Grandma. All of them are good.\n\nGood isn’t the question, Grandma said. Right is the question. Something can be good and still be wrong for you, because it keeps you from what you’re for. Who made you, Imani?\n\nThe Lord.\n\nThen ask Him what He made you for, before you ask the club fair.\n\nThat night Imani made a list of everything she had signed up for and, beside each, wrote a word: for, or not for. She prayed over it, which she realized she had not done in August. In January she dropped four things. She kept tutoring kids in the neighborhood near campus, because that was the thing she had loved since she was twelve.\n\nJalen kept riding to Texas every Thanksgiving. He has never arrived. Everyone agrees that is fine for a rocking horse.',
