        title: 'Every Committee But One',
        body: 'Deacon Raymond Bethel served on every committee at Greater Hope. Trustee board, building fund, men’s ministry, the parking team, the scholarship committee, the anniversary planning group. He had a key to every door in the building. People said, If you need something done, ask Brother Bethel.\n\nHis wife, Gloria, had stopped asking. For eleven years she had said, gently and then less gently, that she missed him, that their son needed his father, that the church could survive one Saturday without him. Raymond always had an answer: it’s the Lord’s work.\n\nIn March Gloria had a stroke. Not a severe one; she would recover most of her speech. Raymond sat by her bed at the hospital for four days without leaving, which was the longest he had stayed anywhere but the church in a decade. On the third night their son, Darnell, now twenty-six, came and sat on the other side of the bed. They did not speak for a long time.\n\nThen Darnell said, You know I joined a different church, right? Two years ago.\n\nRaymond did not know.\n\nI didn’t leave because of the Lord, Darnell said. I left because I couldn’t watch you love that building more than you loved her.\n\nRaymond did not defend himself. In the morning, when the chaplain came by, he asked her to read the story of Martha and Mary. He had preached on it once at a men’s breakfast. This time he heard the line about being careful and troubled about many things, and he wept.\n\nWhen Gloria came home, Raymond resigned from four committees. Some people at Greater Hope were offended. Pastor was not. He said, Brother Bethel, the Lord did not make you to be a key ring. He made you a husband and a father first. Everything else is second.\n\nRaymond still holds one key. He uses it on Sunday mornings to open the door for the early prayer meeting, and then he drives home to pick up Gloria.',
        verse: 'Proverbs 19:21',
